  "question": "1. Background / Context\n\nThis project involves developing an OAuth 2 provider integrated with Ruby on Rails applications, facilitated by the Doorkeeper gem. Doorkeeper is designed to simplify the implementation of OAuth 2 provider capabilities within Ruby on Rails or Grape applications.\n\n2. Objectives\n\nThe primary objective is to provide a robust OAuth 2.0 authorization framework that supports various OAuth flows and features, ensuring secure and efficient authorization processes for applications and their users.\n\n3. Stakeholders & Users\n\n- Developers: Integrate OAuth 2 provider capabilities into their Ruby on Rails or Grape applications using Doorkeeper.\n- Application Users: Benefit from secure authentication and authorization mechanisms provided by the OAuth 2 framework.\n- Application Owners: Manage OAuth applications and monitor authorization processes.\n\n4. Functional Descriptions\n\n- OAuth 2.0 Authorization Framework: Implement a comprehensive OAuth 2 authentication and authorization mechanism.\n- Authorization Code Flow: Allow clients to exchange an authorization code for an access token.\n- Access Token Scopes: Define and enforce access scopes for tokens.\n- Refresh Token: Enable token renewal without requiring re-authentication.\n- Implicit Grant: Support token issuance directly via the authorization response.\n- Resource Owner Password Credentials: Authenticate using resource owner's username and password.\n- Client Credentials: Allow clients to authenticate using their own credentials.\n- OAuth 2.0 Token Revocation: Ensure tokens can be invalidated when needed.\n- OAuth 2.0 Token Introspection: Provide insights into active tokens.\n- OAuth 2.0 Threat Model and Security Considerations: Address potential vulnerabilities and security measures.\n- OAuth 2.0 for Native Apps: Support secure authorization in native client applications.\n- Proof Key for Code Exchange by OAuth Public Clients: Enhance security for public clients using PKCE.\n\n5. Non-Functional Descriptions\n\n- Scalability: Support multiple concurrent OAuth flows as needed by deployed applications.\n- Security: Ensure high-security standards are followed, particularly in token handling and storage.\n- Usability: Provide a developer-friendly interface for integrating OAuth 2 provider functionality into applications.\n- Maintainability: Design the system to facilitate easy updates and modifications, leveraging the Ruby on Rails framework.",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0069\nCREATE TABLE `oauth_applications` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `uid` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `secret` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `redirect_uri` text NOT NULL,\n  `scopes` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `confidential` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT '1',\n  `owner_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `owner_type` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  UNIQUE KEY `index_oauth_applications_on_uid` (`uid`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\nCREATE TABLE `oauth_access_grants` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `resource_owner_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `application_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `token` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `expires_in` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `redirect_uri` text NOT NULL,\n  `scopes` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `revoked_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `code_challenge` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `code_challenge_method` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  UNIQUE KEY `index_oauth_access_grants_on_token` (`token`),\n  KEY `index_oauth_access_grants_on_resource_owner_id` (`resource_owner_id`),\n  KEY `index_oauth_access_grants_on_application_id` (`application_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `oauth_access_grants_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`resource_owner_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `oauth_access_grants_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`application_id`) REFERENCES `oauth_applications` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\nCREATE TABLE `oauth_access_tokens` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `resource_owner_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `application_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `token` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `refresh_token` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `expires_in` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `revoked_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `scopes` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `previous_refresh_token` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  UNIQUE KEY `index_oauth_access_tokens_on_token` (`token`),\n  UNIQUE KEY `index_oauth_access_tokens_on_refresh_token` (`refresh_token`),\n  KEY `index_oauth_access_tokens_on_resource_owner_id` (`resource_owner_id`),\n  KEY `index_oauth_access_tokens_on_application_id` (`application_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `oauth_access_tokens_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`resource_owner_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `oauth_access_tokens_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`application_id`) REFERENCES `oauth_applications` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
